    "summary": "In his new term's inaugural address, Ethiopian Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed stated his government will not back down from its pursuit of maritime access, a stance that has raised concerns among neighboring countries.",
    "content": "Ethiopian Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed declared on Monday that his government will not abandon its efforts to secure sea access, speaking at the swearing-in ceremony for his new term. The statement reaffirms a policy that has been a central pillar of his administration since 2018, when he first took office.\n\nAbiy has repeatedly argued that Ethiopia, as a landlocked nation of over 120 million people, has a legitimate right to maritime access. In 2023, he suggested that Red Sea access was an existential issue, sparking tensions with Eritrea and Djibouti, both of which border Ethiopia and control key ports. His recent comments signal continuity rather than a new direction, but they come amid ongoing regional instability.\n\nThe Horn of Africa is already a tinderbox of conflicts, including the war in Sudan and ongoing tensions in Somalia. Ethiopia's quest for sea access has raised fears of renewed conflict with Eritrea, which gained independence from Ethiopia in 1993 and has since been wary of its larger neighbor's ambitions. Djibouti, which hosts foreign military bases and relies on port revenues, has also expressed unease.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, these developments are particularly concerning. Somalia has long contested Ethiopia's 2024 memorandum of understanding with Somaliland, which would grant Ethiopia sea access in exchange for potential recognition of Somaliland's independence. Many Somalis view this as a violation of their territorial integrity and a threat to regional stability. The diaspora, which remains deeply engaged in Somali politics, has been vocal in opposing any deal that undermines Somalia's sovereignty.\n\nAbiy's latest remarks are likely to inflame these sentiments and complicate ongoing mediation efforts. The international community, including the African Union and the United Nations, has urged dialogue, but little progress has been made. As Ethiopia pushes forward, the risk of escalation remains high, with potentially severe consequences for the entire region and its diaspora.\n\nRead the original report from Horseed Media here: https://horseedmedia.net/abiy-ahmed-oo-ku-dhawaaqay-inuusan-ka-tanaasuli-doonin-doonista-marin-biyood/426246/",
